Dr. Eleanor Vance wasn't a doctor in the traditional sense. Her patients were windows, and her diagnosis was grime. For twenty years, Eleanor had been a window cleaner, a profession she'd stumbled into after a rather disastrous attempt at being a botanist. While the botanical world had proven too delicate for her, the stubbornness of dirt and the satisfying clarity of a clean pane had proven a perfect match.

Eleanor wasn't just any window cleaner. She was meticulous, almost obsessive. She didn't just wipe; she observed. She noticed the way the light refracted, the patterns of water spots, the tiny, almost invisible layers of dust. She knew the difference between bird droppings and pollen, and the best way to remove each. Her clients, a diverse mix of businesses and private homes, appreciated her dedication. They called her "The Clarity Specialist."

Today, her appointment was at the Sterling & Sterling Law Firm, a towering glass and steel structure that dominated the city skyline. It was a challenging job, requiring a harness, a boom lift, and a steady hand. As she ascended in the lift, the city spread out below her – a chaotic tapestry of cars, buildings, and people. Eleanor didn't find it chaotic;
